            <h1 id="intro-heading">About Me</h1>
            <p>
                Hi! I'm Xinyi, I'm a fourth-year Math-CS and Cognitive Science major (Design and Interaction specialization)
                and a researcher at the XR Lab, and interned as a UX researcher at Transsion.
                I'm interested in using design and AI to make the sustainable choice.
                I enjoy thinking critically about a problem and helping teams turn ideas into clear, workable concepts.
            </p>
        </section>

        <hr>

        <!-- My story -->
        <section aria-labelledby="story-heading">
            <h2 id="story-heading">My story</h2>
            <img src="assets/images/mypic-ph.webp" alt="[personal picture]" width="786" height="512">
            <p>
                I came to UC San Diego for Math-CS because I liked problems with clear answers.
                Then I took my first Cognitive Science class and realized the harder problems were about people:
                why we skip the option we know is better, or give up on a tool that was supposed to help us.
                I added Cognitive Science, with a specialization in Design and Interaction, so I could study both sides.
            </p>
            <p>
                At the XR Lab, I work on projects like Immersive Touch, a wearable that turns rehabilitation exercises
                into AR/VR play. As a UX research intern at Transsion, I learned how to test ideas with real users
                and turn what they say into decisions a team can act on.
            </p>
            <p>
                Across all of it, I keep coming back to the same question:
                <i>why is the sustainable option always the harder one?</i>
                I want to keep designing tools, with and without AI, that make the better choice the easier one.
            </p>
        </section>

